## Where the plan goes

Write the plan as the project `{{ plan }}` in the `authoring` source, titled with the
brief's H1, `{{ plan_title }}`, since the navigation lists its runs by that title: its
description rendered from the `plan-description` template and each task from the `plan-task` template,
both as `onepipeline template resolve <name> --json` resolves them, piped into
`onetaskgraph project create` and `onetaskgraph task create` with `--template-loader -`.
Give every task the `orchestrator.unit` metadata naming the unit it changes, and its
`orchestrator.budgets` record as its `budgets` answer. The brief above is
`{{ brief }}`.

**Spikes.** When a decision needs a measurement before it can be planned, create the spikes
project `{{ plan }}-spikes` from this host's `templates/planning/spikes.md.j2` (answer `plan`
with `{{ plan }}` and `native` with the part after its source, title it with the plan's
title plus ` spikes`, `{{ plan_title }} spikes`, and give it
`--metadata onepipeline.schema_version=3`), then add one lifecycle task per spike to it, with the node id
`spike-<topic>`, `onepipeline.publish: "preserve"`, its `repo` and `execution_checkout`, and
no dependency on any member of the spikes template: its only dependency inside the spikes
project is a harness spike it builds on, where it has one. With each spike, add a
`depends_on` edge from that project's `finalize` member onto it (`onetaskgraph task update`
with every spike's `--depends-on`), and none onto the spike-brief member: `finalize` reworks
the plan from the spikes' reports once they have all run, and the spikes run re-runs it
behind any spike whose task changes. Each spike writes its report as the document `<native id>-<spike id>-report` of
`{{ plan }}` from the `spike-report` template. Answer each task of `{{ plan }}` that builds on
a spike with its `spikes` entries now, one `{spike, report, branch}` per spike: the report
that document's id, and the branch the spikes step's branch template names,
`<native id>/<spike id>`. `finalize` reworks exactly the tasks whose stored `spikes` answer
names a spike that changed and never infers which tasks build on one, so a task answered
with no spike is never reworked from a report. A plan that needs no measurement creates no
spikes project, and a plan with no spikes project runs no spikes.

**Replanning a plan its run has started.** When `{{ plan }}` already holds a copy in
`{{ destination }}`, the `refresh` step before this draft brought it to the run's current
state: each task holds its board task's status, and a task a live `add` created is in the
plan. Read each task's `onepipeline.amendment` from that board copy, the item its
`onetaskgraph.copies` entry for `{{ destination }}` names: `onetaskgraph task show <that item>
--json | jq '.items[0].item.metadata["onepipeline.amendment"]'`. An amendment is the run's
binding ruling on its task, which stays as it is: never restate it in the task's text, which
the relaunch would then apply twice. The keys a run writes, those
`onepipeline plan writeback-keys --json` names — settlements, landings, dispatches, amendments among
them — are the run's record, never written into the plan.

**Repositories that span visibilities.** When the brief's `Repositories:` line names
repositories `onevcs boundary inspect` reads as both public and private, run
`onepipeline boundary guidance` and write the plan as it says: one private project in
`authoring`, created with `--classification private`, each public node named in neutral
words and each node rendered with its `boundary` answer.

